        prompt: 'CASE STUDY 1 — Explain the attribution gap to a non-technical client.\n\nBloomfield Garden Centre is a family-run garden centre on the edge of Norwich, selling plants, tools and garden furniture, with an online shop and a large on-site café. Your contact is Denise, one of the owners. She is sharp, busy, and has no marketing background at all. She has just opened two tabs side by side and rung you.\n\n"Facebook says we got 61 purchases in March. Google Analytics says 24. One of you is lying to me. Which is it?"\n\nThe figures for March (all illustrative):\n\n  Meta Ads Manager\n    Purchases reported:            61\n    Attribution setting:           7-day click, 1-day view\n    Of those 61: 38 click-through, 23 view-through\n    Spend:                         £1,150\n\n  GA4 (Traffic acquisition, Paid Social)\n    Sessions:                      2,240\n    Key events (purchase):         24\n    GA4 attribution:               data-driven, default\n\n  Shopify order system (the shop\'s own records)\n    Total online orders in March:  147\n    Total online revenue:          £9,380\n    Orders in February:            131\n\n  Other facts you have established:\n    - The cookie consent banner is set to require opt-in. Roughly\n      a third of visitors decline.\n    - 41% of the website\'s traffic is mobile; many customers browse\n      on a phone and buy later on a desktop.\n    - The Meta pixel and the GA4 tag both fire on the order\n      confirmation page. You tested both and each fires once.\n    - Denise also sends a monthly email and ranks well for\n      "garden centre norwich".\n\nYour deliverable. Write the reply you would send Denise. It must be readable by someone with no marketing knowledge, and it must not blame either tool.\n\nInclude:\n1. A plain-English explanation of why both numbers can be honest, using an everyday comparison rather than jargon.\n2. A short breakdown of which specific factors in the data above account for the gap, in order of how much each probably contributes.\n3. A clear statement of which number you would report as the headline, and why.\n4. What role, if any, the Meta figure should keep in future reporting.\n5. What you would do next month to answer the question Denise is really asking, which is whether the £1,150 was worth spending.\n6. One thing this data genuinely cannot tell you, stated plainly.\n\nWhat a good answer looks like:\n- It identifies the 23 view-through purchases as the single largest cause, and explains view-through without using the phrase as jargon.\n- It names the consent gap and cross-device buying as reducing the GA4 figure, rather than treating GA4 as simply wrong.\n- It refuses to add platform figures together and anchors the headline on the 147 orders from the order system.\n- It gives Meta\'s number a defined, limited job: comparing Meta ads with other Meta ads.\n- It proposes a holdout or geo test as the only way to establish whether the spend was incremental.\n- It contains no promise of results and no suggestion that either tool should be switched off or "fixed".\n\nLength guide: roughly 450-600 words, written as an email Denise will actually read.',
